Your build works. It got through the MLIR pipeline and failed at the last stage, where it hands off to `hivmc`, the external Bisheng backend that turns IR into an actual device binary. That ships with CANN, it isn't built from this repo.

Find it:

```bash
ls /usr/local/ascend-toolkit/ 2>/dev/null
ls /data/pri/Ascend/latest/ascend-toolkit/ 2>/dev/null
find / -name "hivmc" -type f 2>/dev/null | head
find / -name "set_env.sh" -path "*ascend*" 2>/dev/null | head
```

If a `set_env.sh` turns up, source it and retry. That's what populates PATH with the CANN compiler tools:

```bash
source <path>/set_env.sh
which hivmc
bishengir-compile add.mlir -enable-hivm-compile -o kernel.o
```

This connects back to the env vars Hocky gave you at the start. `BISHENG_INSTALL_PATH=/data/ci_env/daily/pkg/ccec/bin` and the `bishengir/bin` path are exactly this piece of the toolchain. Those paths were for the other server, but if fremont mounts the same `/data`, check:

```bash
ls /data/ci_env/daily/pkg/ccec/bin 2>/dev/null
ls /data/pri/ 2>/dev/null
```

Note that `/data` may exist on the host but not be mounted into the container. Check from the Fedora side too if it comes up empty here.

If CANN isn't on fremont at all, that's a question for Hocky: whether you're meant to install the toolkit in your container, or do device compilation on the other machine. Paste the results of those finds.
